<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/MMLib.Alvo.Host/MMLib.Alvo.Host.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The shared response readers, linked rather than re-implemented. A host fact reading a body by hand
    (`body.ShouldContain("W-1")`) passes on a bare JSON array, on a row moved into a diagnostic field, and on
    any response whose status nobody checked; ResponseReading exists precisely to refuse a body it does not
    recognise — its own remarks call a quiet reader "a vacuity generator for every fact written after it".
    Only the readers are linked, not the whole of test/_shared/api: the API world there builds its own
    embedded WebApplication, and this suite's fixture must stay AlvoHost's own two methods.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../_shared/api/ResponseReading.cs" Link="_shared/ResponseReading.cs" />

    <!--
      And the faulting store, for the same reason: family 5 is unreachable from a well-formed request by
      design, and since #138 mapped the last real-store failure that reached it onto a 409, a fact about a
      500 has to substitute one. Re-implementing it here would be a second store whose failure family could
      drift from the one the core's suite measures.
    -->
    <Compile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../_shared/api/FaultingAlvoData.cs" Link="_shared/FaultingAlvoData.cs" />
    <Using Include="MMLib.Alvo.Api.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="descriptors/*.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
